      ":question/id": "9e8b61bc-bb4e-5bb5-ab6c-973e00e2baa2",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-13647",
      ":question/problem": "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "If we let $u=4x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
