      ":question/id": "60a6f7c5-9743-5406-97a4-9c3469a873e7",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-350040",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}9\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "If we let $u=2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}9\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
