      ":question/problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}6\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "If we let $u=\\frac{2\\pi x}{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}6\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}6\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u\\cdot \\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\pi }(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
